<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Philae</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Philae is an island in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> near Aswan, at the southern frontier of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, famed for its temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>, begun under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nectanebo-i">Nectanebo I</a> and built mainly under the Ptolemies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a>. The sanctuary drew worshippers from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a>, including the Blemmyes, until its closure under Justinian around 537. When the Aswan dams flooded the island, its temples were moved in the 1970s to the nearby island of Agilkia.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/philae.jpg" alt="Philae" width="320" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Holger Uwe Schmitt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1977_bis_1980_wurde_die_Tempel_auf_der_Insel_Philae_auf_die_h%C3%B6her_gelegene_Insel_Agilka_versetzt._01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Philae lay just above the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-cataract">First Cataract</a> near Aswan. Before the floods it was no more than 380 metres long and about 120 metres broad, a steep island of syenite ringed by a high wall. Its ancient Egyptian name meant boundary, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharaoh">pharaohs</a> kept a strong garrison there, later followed by Greek and Roman soldiers. Because the cataract was seldom navigable, goods traded between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nubia">Nubia</a> were landed and re-embarked at Syene and Philae, and the island was a centre of commerce between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meroe">Meroë</a> and Memphis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>More than two thirds of the surviving buildings date from the Ptolemaic era, when Philae overtook Elephantine as the main sanctuary of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and drew pilgrims from as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a> and the Greek mainland. Inscriptions left by three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> in 116 BCE are the oldest known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> inscriptions in Egypt. The Nubian kings Arqamani and Adikhalamani also added to the temples, which some scholars read as collaboration with the Ptolemaic government and others as a Nubian occupation. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diocletian">Diocletian</a> built a triple arch and in 298 drew the frontier back to about Philae itself.</p><p>The last known inscription in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egyptian-hieroglyphs">Egyptian hieroglyphs</a> was carved there in 394 and the last known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demotic-egyptian-script">Demotic</a> one in 452. Christians and temple worship coexisted for a time, and a treaty of 452 between the Roman commander Maximinus and the Blemmyes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobades">Nobades</a> guaranteed access to the image of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isis">Isis</a>. Procopius records the closure under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>, carried out by the local commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narses">Narses</a> the Persarmenian; the scholar Jitse Dijkstra argues that organised paganism there had already ended in the 5th century. Five temples became churches, the Temple of Isis being dedicated to Saint Stephen.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>After the Aswan Low Dam of 1902 the island was flooded for most of each year. When the High Dam threatened it entirely, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> and an Egyptian design won out: a coffer dam was built, the monuments were recorded by photogrammetry, cut into about 40,000 blocks and rebuilt from 1977 on Agilkia, about 500 metres away, levelled to Philae's old shape. Italian companies did the work under the architect Giovanni Joppolo. The reliefs are being published by the Philae Temple Text Project of the Austrian Academy of Sciences.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Philae temple complex” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Egypt was lost to Palmyra under Zenobia around 270 and recovered by Aurelian, and Diocletian besieged Alexandria to crush a usurpation in 297–298 and withdrew the southern frontier to <b>Philae</b> in the face of the Blemmyes and Nobades.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The temples of the Ptolemaic period, at Edfu, Dendera and <b>Philae</b>, preserve on their walls the most complete records of temple rituals, festivals and myths.</blockquote><a href="/religion/ancient-egyptian-religion">Ancient Egyptian religion · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote>The last hieroglyphic inscription was carved at <b>Philae</b> in 394 CE, and the last demotic one in 452.</blockquote><a href="/language/ancient-egyptian">Ancient Egyptian · Speakers &amp; Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Its success helped the United Nations introduce a convention establishing the UNESCO World Heritage List, and the relocated complex belongs, with <b>Philae</b>, Amada and Wadi es-Sebua, to the World Heritage Site known as the Nubian Monuments.</blockquote><a href="/work/abu-simbel">Abu Simbel · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Knowledge of hieroglyphs had faded by the 4th century CE; the last known hieroglyphic inscription, at <b>Philae</b>, dates from 394.</blockquote><a href="/work/rosetta-stone">Rosetta Stone · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>…teach, and the Platonic school of Athens closed; around 537 his officials closed the temple of Isis at <b>Philae</b>, on Egypt's southern frontier, where priests had served the goddess for the Nubian peoples beyond the border and where the last known hieroglyphic inscription had been carved in 394.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li></ul></section>
